<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/expert/ethics-privacy-and-bias">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Data ethics is <strong>the study of harm data systems cause — and the design choices that
    prevent it</strong>. Bias, privacy erosion, opacity and consent gaps are not edge cases;
    they are default behaviours of inattentive systems. The professional skill is seeing the
    harms early enough that fixing them is a design change, not a scandal response. The reason
    this sits inside data science itself — not in a compliance annex — is structural: a model is
    a decision factory. Every threshold it sets, every applicant it ranks, every risk score it
    emits is a decision about a person, made at a scale and speed no human review can audit. The
    discipline's task is to make those decisions as examined as the code that produces them.
  </p>
  <p>
    The expert framing is neither "algorithms are biased, ban them" nor "maths is neutral". A
    neutral algorithm applied to an unequal world reproduces the inequality with better
    bookkeeping, and the fix is never just technical: fairness definitions encode policy
    choices, privacy budgets trade individual protection against collective knowledge, and
    consent design is a power question wearing a UX costume. This chapter works through the
    mechanisms with the cases that taught the field its caution.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four problem areas and their working fixes" widget="equationStep" config={ethicsCfg} />

  <h2>What data science is for — the responsibility layer</h2>
  <p>
    The introductory framing of this course (Data 8's opening chapter) defines data science as
    the full cycle — question, data, exploration, inference, communication — <em>plus</em> a
    standing obligation to study the impact of conclusions on the people the data describes.
    That obligation is not decoration; it is the difference between statistics as mathematics and
    statistics as practice. Every stage of the cycle embeds ethical decisions: which question
    gets asked (and which gets funded), which population is sampled (and who is invisible in the
    frame), which outcome is predicted (arrests, which reflect policing, rather than crime),
    which metric counts as success (clicks, which reward outrage). The professional habit this
    chapter builds is running that audit <em>before</em> deployment, because the asymmetry of
    data systems runs one way: a thousand users can be harmed in the minute a model is live, and
    none of them can see the model that did it. With great inferential power comes
    accountability to the described — the field's translation of an older principle.
  </p>

  <h2>Bias in, bias out: how models inherit discrimination</h2>
  <p>
    The mechanism is worth stating precisely, because it is boring and therefore everywhere.
    Supervised models learn patterns from historical labels; if the labels encode historical
    decisions, the model learns the decisions, including the discriminatory ones — and then
    applies them with machine consistency. <strong>COMPAS</strong>, the recidivism risk score
    used in US courts, is the canonical case. ProPublica (2016) analysed roughly 7,000 Broward
    County, Florida defendants and found Black defendants who did <em>not</em> reoffend were
    flagged high-risk at nearly twice the rate of white defendants who did not reoffend — a
    false-positive rate of about 45% versus 23%. The vendor's rebuttal was also true: the score
    was <em>calibrated</em>, meaning a 7 meant the same recidivism probability for both groups.
    Both things held simultaneously, because the underlying arrest rates differed — the maths
    let everyone be right, which is the deepest lesson of the case. Amazon's experimental
    recruiting engine (reported 2018) is the same mechanism in a different organ: trained on ten
    years of predominantly male hires, it learned to downgrade CVs containing the word
    "women's" — it was never deployed, and its speed of harm was the point: a biased human
    reviewer tires; a biased model reviews millions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "just remove race and gender from the inputs and the model
    can't discriminate." Proxies reconstruct what you delete: postcode predicts race in
    segregated cities, shopping patterns predict pregnancy (Target's famous case), name predicts
    gender. Deletion is not a fix; measurement is — evaluate error rates per group, audit the
    proxy set, and treat the model's decisions, not its inputs, as the object of scrutiny.
  </p>

  <h2>Fairness definitions conflict — and that is a theorem</h2>
  <p>
    The reason fairness is hard is not vagueness; it is arithmetic. The main candidate criteria
    are: <strong>demographic parity</strong> (the same fraction of each group is selected),
    <strong>equalised odds</strong> (the same false-positive and false-negative rates in each
    group), and <strong>calibration</strong> (a score of 7 means the same risk for everyone).
    Kleinberg, Mullainathan and Raghavan (2016) and Chouldechova (2017) independently proved that
    when base rates differ between groups, calibration and equalised odds are
    <em>mathematically incompatible</em> — you cannot have both, except in degenerate cases. So
    COMPAS's vendor and ProPublica were each holding one horn of a proved dilemma. The
    consequence reshapes practice: choosing a fairness metric is a <strong>policy decision about
    which errors matter</strong>, to be made with stakeholders and documented, not a technical
    default to be coded at 2 a.m. A bail-setting system might reasonably prefer equal
    false-positive rates (liberty wrongly denied); a marketing system might accept parity of
    selection. What the field forbids is not any particular choice — it is the
    <em>unexamined</em> choice.
  </p>

  <h2>Privacy beyond names: re-identification is the default</h2>
  <p>
    "Anonymised" has failed so many times that the burden of proof has flipped. The mechanism of
    failure is <strong>quasi-identifiers</strong>: combinations of innocuous fields that are
    jointly unique. Latanya Sweeney showed that about 87% of the US population is unique on just
    three fields — ZIP code, date of birth, sex — and demonstrated it by re-identifying the
    medical record of the Governor of Massachusetts from a "de-identified" state employee
    dataset. <strong>AOL, 2006:</strong> the company released 20 million search queries from
    650,000 users with IDs but no names; within days, journalists re-identified user 4417749 —
    a 62-year-old woman from Lilburn, Georgia — from her own queries about her health and her
    neighbours. <strong>Netflix, 2007:</strong> the Prize dataset of 500,000 users' film ratings
    was defeated by Narayanan and Shmatikov, who matched users against public IMDb reviews with
    startling accuracy. The lesson generalises: high-dimensional data (search history, ratings,
    location pings) is a fingerprint, and joining two datasets defeats most naive masking.
    k-anonymity (every record identical to at least k − 1 others on the identifying fields) was
    the classic defence and is demonstrably insufficient — homogeneity attacks (everyone in the
    bucket shares the sensitive value) and background knowledge break it.
  </p>

  <h2>Differential privacy: the mathematics of plausible denial</h2>
  <p>
    The field's strongest answer, due to Cynthia Dwork and colleagues (2006), changes the goal:
    instead of trying to scrub identifiers, add <strong>calibrated noise</strong> so that the
    published result is statistically almost identical whether or not any one person's data is
    included. The guarantee is parameterised by a privacy budget epsilon: smaller epsilon means
    more noise and stronger protection, and composing many queries spends the budget down —
    which turns privacy into an explicit, budgetable resource rather than a hope. The mechanism
    has survived contact with reality at three historic scales: Apple uses local differential
    privacy for iOS usage statistics; Google for browser telemetry; and the 2020 US Census
    applied it to the decennial redistricting data, accepting measurable noise to protect
    330 million people from reconstruction attacks (researchers had already shown the 2010
    release's tabulations were re-identifiable from public records). The design conversation is
    always the same trade — how much accuracy a use case can give up for how much protection —
    but it is now a conversation with units, budgets and proofs.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "adding noise makes the data useless." It makes the data
    <em>bounded</em>: aggregate questions retain excellent accuracy while individual-level
    questions lose meaning — which is usually exactly the right trade. The census still
    redistricts; hospitals still publish; Apple still ships keyboards. What dies is the fantasy
    of free, perfect, person-level data.
  </p>

  <h2>Consent, power and the legal floor</h2>
  <p>
    Consent, in practice, is asymmetric: users click "accept" on policies measured in thousands
    of words that they cannot negotiate and would not read at any length — EU studies have found
    only a small minority of users actually read terms, and eye-tracking work shows average
    reading time per policy would take hundreds of hours a year. So regulation has moved the
    baseline from notice to <strong>rights</strong>. The GDPR (in force 2018) established lawful
    bases for processing, data minimisation, purpose limitation, and enforceable rights of
    access, correction, deletion and portability, with fines up to 20 million euros or 4% of
    global turnover — a number that moved ethics from aspiration to line item. California's
    CCPA/CPRA and similar statutes extended the pattern. The expert distinction: compliance is
    the floor, not the ethics. A consent flow can be lawful and still a dark pattern; a data
    use can be permitted and still corrosive. The professional question — would the person
    described by this data consider this use fair if they could see it? — is not in any statute,
    and it catches what the statutes miss.
  </p>

  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bias mitigation is a patch you ship once." The world
  under the model keeps moving: populations shift, adversarial users adapt, upstream
  collection practices change silently — a model audited fair at launch can drift unfair
  within a year with no code change at all. That is why the mature practice treats audits
  like financial controls: scheduled, versioned, owned, and re-run.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The impossibility results, formally.</strong> The fairness-conflict theorem has a
    clean sketch: with differing base rates, equal false-positive and false-negative rates force
    the same <em>precision</em> only if positive predictive value is also equalised — but
    precision then differs unless selection rates track base rates, which breaks parity. Each
    proposed resolution (equalising different triples, conditional fairness given legitimate
    factors, counterfactual fairness defined over causal models) re-chooses which quantity to
    hold fixed; the research frontier is causal formulations — asking what the decision
    <em>would have been</em> had the protected attribute differed — which reframes fairness as a
    question about the world's causal structure, not the confusion matrix.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Attacks on models themselves.</strong> Privacy risk now extends into trained
    systems: membership-inference attacks determine whether a specific person's record was in the
    training set; model-inversion and reconstruction attacks recover training data from large
    language models (researchers have extracted verbatim personal text from published models);
    memorisation scales with duplication. Defences (differential privacy in training, federated
    learning, machine unlearning) each trade accuracy and engineering cost, and evaluating the
    trade honestly is a current research discipline rather than a solved checklist.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Governance as the frontier.</strong> The maturing practice is organisational, not
    algorithmic: model cards and datasheets that document intended use and evaluated subgroups;
    algorithmic impact assessments modelled on environmental ones; red-teaming and external
    audits as a profession (with standards emerging from NIST's AI Risk Management Framework and
    the EU AI Act's conformity regime). The open debate is accountability structure — who is
    liable when a scaled decision harms, and whether audit access can be mandated without
    compromising the very privacy the systems promise to protect. Every case in this chapter
    began as someone's unexamined default; the governance layer exists to make sure the next
    ones begin as someone's documented decision.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Angwin et al., ProPublica (2016); Narayanan & Shmatikov (2008) on de-anonymisation; Dwork & Roth, "The Algorithmic Foundations of Differential Privacy"; EU GDPR text.</p>
